CONCEPTS=[
('1','fa-leaf','Biodiversity and Why It Matters',r'''<p><strong>Biodiversity</strong> means the immense variety of living organisms and their forms and habitats. It is not simply a list of species: organisms are interconnected and each can contribute to ecosystem stability.</p><div class="sj-grid"><div class="sj-grid-card"><h4>Ecological value</h4><p>Algae release oxygen; fungi and bacteria decompose waste; birds, bees and bats pollinate; plants capture sunlight and support food chains.</p></div><div class="sj-grid-card"><h4>Human value</h4><p>Biodiversity supports food, shelter, medicines and livelihoods. Crop diversity can reduce the risk of crop failure and strengthen food security.</p></div></div><p><strong>Exam idea:</strong> when asked why biodiversity matters, connect <em>variety → ecosystem functions → stability → human dependence</em>.</p>'''),
('2','fa-map-marked-alt','India as a Biodiversity Hotspot',r'''<p>India contains mountains, deserts, rainforests, plateaus and long coastlines, creating many habitats with different soils, temperatures and rainfall. Some organisms occur naturally only in a particular region; these are <strong>endemic species</strong>.</p><p>Examples in the chapter include Nilgiri tahr, lion-tailed macaque, <em>Nepenthes khasiana</em> and Neelakurinji. A <strong>biodiversity hotspot</strong> is a region with many endemic species that has also experienced significant habitat loss.</p><div class="sj-detail"><h4>Hotspots named in the chapter</h4><p>Western Ghats; Indo-Burma including North East India; Himalayas; Sundaland including the Nicobar Islands.</p></div>'''),
('3','fa-dna','How Biodiversity Evolved',r'''<p>The chapter explains biodiversity as the outcome of continuous change over a vast span of time. Small differences among individuals can affect survival and reproduction under changing conditions. Differences that accumulate over generations can give rise to new forms of life.</p><p>Thus, present-day diversity reflects interactions between organisms and their surroundings over long periods. Classification provides a systematic framework for studying this diversity.</p>'''),
('4','fa-sitemap','How to Classify Organisms',r'''<p>Classification is a systematic way of organising living diversity. The same organism can fit into different groups if different criteria are chosen; scientists therefore use characteristics that reveal meaningful similarities and differences.</p><table class="sj-table"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>What is examined?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>External features</td><td>Shape, size and body organisation</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Autotrophic or heterotrophic</td></tr><tr><td>Internal structure</td><td>Skeleton, organs and tissues</td></tr><tr><td>Cell structure</td><td>Unicellular/multicellular, prokaryote/eukaryote, cell wall</td></tr><tr><td>Ecological role</td><td>Producer, consumer or decomposer</td></tr><tr><td>Reproduction</td><td>Asexual and/or sexual methods</td></tr><tr><td>Genetic similarity</td><td>Inherited features studied using DNA</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Similar features may suggest common ancestry.</p>'''+check('Pause and Ponder','If many organisms share common features, could they also share a common ancestry?','Yes. Shared characteristics can indicate evolutionary relationships or common ancestry; genetic evidence provides deeper information about such relationships.')),
('5','fa-book-open','Need for Biological Classification',r'''<p>With millions of organisms, unorganised information would be difficult to study. Classification makes the study systematic, helps compare similarities and differences, helps understand relationships and interactions, supports identification and naming, and assists biodiversity conservation.</p><div class="sj-detail"><h4>Pakke Tiger Reserve case</h4><p>The chapter describes nearly 300 bird species in Pakke and four hornbill species associated with large old trees and specific fruits. Classification helps scientists track species, distinguish similar organisms and understand habitat links.</p></div>'''),
('6','fa-history','Classification Systems Through Time',r'''<p>Classification changed as scientific knowledge improved.</p><table class="sj-table"><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Key development</th><th>Time / scientist</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Artificial system</td><td>Animals grouped mainly by habitat and external appearance</td><td>Aristotle, 4th century BCE</td></tr><tr><td>Two kingdom</td><td>Plantae and Animalia</td><td>18th century / Linnaeus</td></tr><tr><td>Three kingdom</td><td>Protista added for microscopic life</td><td>1866 / Haeckel</td></tr><tr><td>Four kingdom</td><td>Monera separated</td><td>1938 / Copeland</td></tr><tr><td>Five kingdom</td><td>Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, Animalia</td><td>1969 / Whittaker</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Why did systems change?</strong> New observations from microscopes and later genetic studies revealed distinctions that older systems could not explain.</p>'''),
('7','fa-bacterium','Five Kingdom Classification: The Big Picture',r'''<p>Whittaker's five kingdoms group organisms using cell type, cell structure, level of organisation, mode of nutrition and ecological role.</p><table class="sj-table"><thead><tr><th>Kingdom</th><th>Core features</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Monera</td><td>Unicellular prokaryotes</td></tr><tr><td>Protista</td><td>Unicellular eukaryotes; diverse nutrition</td></tr><tr><td>Fungi</td><td>Mostly multicellular eukaryotes; chitin wall; heterotrophic by absorption</td></tr><tr><td>Plantae</td><td>Multicellular eukaryotes; cellulose wall; autotrophic/photosynthetic</td></tr><tr><td>Animalia</td><td>Multicellular eukaryotes; heterotrophic; no cell wall</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>High-value comparison:</strong> do not classify solely by habitat. Cellular organisation is more fundamental because unrelated organisms can share a habitat.</p>'''),
('8','fa-microscope','Kingdom Monera',r'''<p>Monera includes bacteria and cyanobacteria, which are single-celled <strong>prokaryotes</strong>. Bacteria occur in soil, water, air, hot springs, extreme environments and inside bodies. Some are pathogenic; others are useful, such as Lactobacillus and Rhizobium.</p><p>Some bacteria participate in nutrient cycling and degradation of pollutants. Bacteria in ruminant gut ecosystems also contribute to biogas production from dung. Cyanobacteria are described as autotrophs and decomposers.</p><div class="sj-detail"><h4>Remember</h4><p><strong>Monera → microscopic + unicellular + prokaryotic.</strong> A true membrane-bound nucleus is absent.</p></div>'''),
('9','fa-water','Kingdom Protista',r'''<p>Protista includes single-celled eukaryotic organisms, generally living in water or moist places. They have a true nucleus. Some are autotrophic and others heterotrophic; therefore nutrition alone cannot define the kingdom.</p><p>Examples shown in the chapter include <strong>Amoeba, Paramecium and Euglena</strong>. Protists can be important in aquatic food chains, oxygen production and nutrient cycling.</p><div class="sj-detail"><h4>Laboratory link</h4><p>The chapter describes a hay infusion activity for observing microscopic protists. It also stresses appropriate laboratory precautions and disposal.</p></div>'''),
('10','fa-seedling','Kingdom Fungi',r'''<p>Fungi are mostly multicellular eukaryotes with <strong>chitin</strong> cell walls. They do not make their own food; many absorb nutrients from dead and decaying matter through fine filaments forming a <strong>mycelium</strong>. Many are saprophytes and act as decomposers.</p><p>Fungi may also be mutualistic or parasitic. They reproduce sexually and asexually, often by spores. Examples include yeast, Aspergillus, Penicillium and mushrooms. Yeast is unicellular but is included in Fungi because of its fungal characteristics such as the chitin cell wall.</p><p>Fungi recycle nutrients and therefore contribute strongly to soil fertility and ecological balance.</p>'''),
('11','fa-tree','Kingdom Plantae and Plant Groups',r'''<p>Plants are multicellular, autotrophic eukaryotes with cellulose cell walls and photosynthetic nutrition. The chapter divides Plantae into five classes: <strong>Thallophyta, Bryophyta, Pteridophyta, Gymnosperm and Angiosperm</strong>.</p><table class="sj-table"><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Key progression</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Thallophyta</td><td>Simple thallus; mainly aquatic/moist; e.g. Spirogyra</td></tr><tr><td>Bryophyta</td><td>Moist land; rhizoids; no vascular tissue; water needed for reproduction</td></tr><tr><td>Pteridophyta</td><td>True roots, stems, leaves; xylem/phloem; no seeds; water needed for reproduction</td></tr><tr><td>Gymnosperm</td><td>Seeds not enclosed in fruits; cones; no aquatic condition needed for fertilisation</td></tr><tr><td>Angiosperm</td><td>Flowers and fruits; seeds enclosed in fruits; diverse and widespread</td></tr></tbody></table>'''),
('12','fa-moss','Thallophyta, Bryophyta and Pteridophyta',r'''<p><strong>Thallophyta:</strong> body is a simple thallus, facilitating direct exchange with surroundings; suited to water/moisture but the chapter notes inability to live on land.</p><p><strong>Bryophyta:</strong> represent early colonisation of land. They have rhizoids and simple stem/leaf-like structures but lack true roots, stems and leaves and lack vascular tissues. They require water for reproduction, hence the term <strong>amphibians of the plant kingdom</strong>.</p><p><strong>Pteridophyta:</strong> have true roots, stems and leaves and vascular tissues—xylem transports water and phloem transports food. They live on land but still depend on water for reproduction and do not produce seeds.</p>'''),
('13','fa-pine-tree','Gymnosperms and Angiosperms',r'''<p><strong>Gymnosperms</strong> such as pine and cycad are adapted to cold and dry conditions. Needle-like or scale-like leaves reduce water loss. They produce seeds that are not enclosed in fruits and are often exposed on cones. Aquatic conditions are not required for fertilisation.</p><p><strong>Angiosperms</strong> are flowering plants with complex body organisation. Flowers attract pollinators and fruits help disperse seeds. Their seeds are enclosed within fruits, allowing them to occupy a wide range of environments.</p><div class="sj-detail"><h4>Evolutionary pattern stated by the chapter</h4><p>From algae to angiosperms, structural changes include better land adaptation, transport tissues, seeds and flowers.</p></div>'''),
('14','fa-spider','Animalia and Invertebrate Phyla',r'''<p>Animals are multicellular heterotrophic eukaryotes. A major classification criterion in the chapter is the presence or absence of a <strong>notochord</strong>. Non-chordates lack it; chordates possess it at least during development, with vertebrates having a vertebral column.</p><table class="sj-table"><thead><tr><th>Phylum</th><th>Key feature</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Porifera</td><td>Cellular level; pores; no true tissues/organs</td><td>Sponges</td></tr><tr><td>Cnidaria</td><td>Tissue level; tentacles; single opening</td><td>Hydra, jellyfish, corals</td></tr><tr><td>Platyhelminthes</td><td>Flat, bilateral body; single opening</td><td>Flatworms</td></tr><tr><td>Nematoda</td><td>Cylindrical body; mouth and anus</td><td>Roundworms</td></tr><tr><td>Annelida</td><td>Segmentation; body cavity; organ-system level</td><td>Earthworm</td></tr><tr><td>Arthropoda</td><td>Jointed appendages; hard exoskeleton</td><td>Insects, crabs, spiders</td></tr><tr><td>Mollusca</td><td>Soft body; often shell; muscular foot</td><td>Snail, squid</td></tr><tr><td>Echinodermata</td><td>Calcium-carbonate internal skeleton</td><td>Starfish, sea urchin</td></tr></tbody></table>'''),
('15','fa-fish','Protochordates and Vertebrates',r'''<p><strong>Protochordates</strong>, such as Amphioxus, possess a notochord at least once during life. The notochord provides internal support without restricting movement and represents an important transition in body organisation.</p><p><strong>Vertebrates</strong> possess a vertebral column that supports the body and protects vital organs such as the brain and spinal cord. The chapter groups vertebrates into <strong>fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals</strong>, using broad patterns of habitat, body covering and reproduction.</p>'''),
('16','fa-feather-alt','Adaptations as Outcomes of Structural Change',r'''<p>Animal diversity reflects different structural solutions to environmental challenges. The chapter gives examples: fins and gills in fish for aquatic movement and respiration; feathers and hollow bones in birds for flight; fat storage in camels and thick fur in polar bears for extreme conditions; mammary glands in mammals for survival of young.</p><p>The key reasoning pattern is <strong>structural feature → function → survival advantage → adaptation to environment</strong>.</p>'''),
('17','fa-layer-group','Hierarchy of Classification',r'''<p>Classification proceeds from broad groups to increasingly specific groups. The order is:</p><div class="sj-detail"><h4>Kingdom → Phylum → Class → Order → Family → Genus → Species</h4><p>At each lower level, organisms share more common features and the group contains fewer organisms. The chapter compares this to an address: each step identifies the organism more precisely.</p></div><p>For the tiger, the chapter gives Animalia → Chordata → Vertebrata → Mammalia → Carnivora → Felidae → Panthera → <em>P. tigris</em>. For pea, it gives Plantae → Magnoliophyta → Magnoliopsida → Fabales → Fabaceae → <em>Pisum</em> → <em>P. sativum</em>.</p>'''),
('18','fa-tag','Binomial Nomenclature',r'''<p><strong>Binomial nomenclature</strong> gives each organism a universal scientific name with two parts. Carolus Linnaeus introduced the system described in the chapter.</p><ol><li>First word = <strong>genus</strong>, begins with a capital letter.</li><li>Second word = <strong>species</strong>, written in lower case.</li><li>Printed scientific names are written in <strong>italics</strong>; when handwritten, they are underlined.</li></ol><p>Examples: <em>Panthera tigris</em> (tiger) and <em>Mangifera indica</em> (mango). A genus contains closely related species sharing common features.</p>'''),
('19','fa-dna','Three Domains and Changing Classification',r'''<p>Five-kingdom classification improved organisation of life but could not fully explain all diversity. Advances in microscopy and genetic studies enabled comparison at the DNA level. The chapter states that Carl Woese proposed the <strong>three-domain system in 1977</strong>: <strong>Bacteria, Archaea and Eukarya</strong>.</p><p>This illustrates that classification is an ongoing scientific process. New evidence can expose limitations of an earlier system and lead to a better framework.</p>'''),
('20','fa-fossil','Fossils and Biodiversity Under Threat',r'''<p><strong>Fossils</strong> are preserved remains of plants and animals found in layers of rocks, sand and mud. The chapter states that older layers generally contain simpler organisms while newer layers show more complex forms; fossils therefore provide natural records of changes in life over millions of years.</p><p>Biodiversity is threatened by human activities including pollution, deforestation, overuse of resources and climate change. Loss of one species can affect other organisms that depend on it.</p><div class="sj-detail"><h4>Conservation case</h4><p>The chapter describes phumdis in Loktak Lake and the endemic Sangai deer of Manipur, whose habitat is degenerating. Habitat loss can significantly affect the deer population, so conservation of both habitat and species is important.</p></div>''')]
_engine.CONCEPTS=CONCEPTS

_engine.NCERT=[
    nq(1,'Meena and Hari observed an animal in their garden. Hari called it an insect while Meena said it was an earthworm. Choose the correct option which confirms that it is an insect.','<p><strong>Answer: (ii) Body with jointed legs.</strong> Jointed legs are a characteristic feature of arthropods, the animal group to which insects belong.</p>','1',['(i) Bilateral symmetrical body', '(ii) Body with jointed legs', '(iii) Cylindrical body', '(iv) Body with little segmentation']),
    nq(2,'Sponges represent one of the simplest animal body plans. Their bodies lack true tissues and organs. Which feature of sponge cells supports its classification under the animal kingdom?','<p><strong>Answer: (iii) Presence of a cell membrane.</strong> Sponge cells have the basic cellular organisation of animal cells; the other options do not support their classification under Animalia.</p>','1',['(i) Absence of mitochondria', '(ii) Ability to photosynthesise', '(iii) Presence of a cell membrane', '(iv) Presence of a cell wall']),
    nq(3,'Observe two different animals in your immediate environment. What features help you distinguish between them? How do these features help place them into different groups?','<p>Observe features such as body symmetry, segmentation, appendages, body covering, skeleton, level of organisation, habitat, mode of nutrition and reproduction. Shared and contrasting characteristics help scientists place organisms into meaningful groups.</p>','2',None),
    nq(4,'How would a scientist justify choosing cellular organisation as a more fundamental characteristic for the basis of classification rather than the presence of xylem and phloem?','<p>Cellular organisation is a broad characteristic applicable to all living organisms. Xylem and phloem are specialised tissues found only in particular plant groups. Therefore, cellular organisation provides a more fundamental basis for major classification.</p>','3',None),
    nq(5,'You find an unlabelled slide of a single-celled organism that has a well-defined nucleus and multiple cilia. Which group would it most likely belong to? Give reasons.','<p><strong>Protista.</strong> The organism is single-celled and has a well-defined nucleus, so it is a unicellular eukaryote. Multiple cilia are consistent with protists such as <em>Paramecium</em>.</p>','3',None),
    nq(6,'How does the diversity of organisms contribute to the balance and stability of an ecosystem?','<p>Different organisms perform different ecological roles. Plants produce food and oxygen, animals pollinate flowers and disperse seeds, while microorganisms recycle nutrients. The interdependence of these roles supports food webs, nutrient cycling and ecosystem stability.</p>','3',None),
    nq(7,'If all unicellular organisms were grouped into a single kingdom, what problems would arise?','<p>Unicellular organisms are not all alike. Some are prokaryotic while others are eukaryotic; their nutrition, cell structures and ecological roles also differ. Placing them all in one kingdom would hide these fundamental differences.</p>','3',None),
    nq(8,'Viruses were studied in earlier classes. Why are they not placed in any of the five kingdoms? Give reasons.','<p>Viruses are acellular and therefore do not possess the cellular organisation used as a fundamental basis of the five-kingdom classification. They contain genetic material but remain inactive outside a host cell.</p>','3',None),
    nq(9,'If you were asked to revise the five kingdom classification, would you create a separate category for viruses or keep them outside the system? Justify your answer and explain what this indicates about the evolving nature of scientific classification.','<p>A justified answer is to keep viruses outside the five-kingdom system because they lack cellular organisation. A revised system could define a suitable category if future evidence supports one. This demonstrates that scientific classification is an evolving process and can change as new evidence becomes available.</p>','3',None),
    nq(10,'Viruses contain genetic material like living organisms but lack cellular organisation. Which features prevent them from fitting into the five kingdom system? What does this tell us about the limitations of classification systems?','<p>Viruses lack cellular organisation and remain inactive outside a host cell, so they do not fit the cellular criteria used for the five kingdoms. This shows that classification systems are models based on selected characteristics and available scientific evidence, and therefore have limitations.</p>','3',None),
    nq(11,'Both pteridophytes and bryophytes lack flowers and seeds, yet they are placed in different groups. Explain this classification using their key features.','<p>Bryophytes lack true roots, stems and leaves and do not have vascular tissues; they require water for reproduction. Pteridophytes have true roots, stems and leaves and possess xylem and phloem, although they still reproduce through spores and require water for reproduction. These structural differences justify their separate groups.</p>','4',None),
    nq(12,'In the classification hierarchy, which group\u2009—\u2009class or genus\u2009—\u2009has fewer members but more features in common? Explain your answer.','<p><strong>Genus.</strong> As we move downward through the classification hierarchy, the number of organisms in a group generally decreases while the number of shared characteristics increases. A genus is therefore more specific than a class.</p>','2',None),
    nq(13,'A scientist discovers a new organism with the characteristic features of locomotion and autotrophic nutrition. Which character(s) would help the scientist identify the organism belonging to Protista according to the five kingdom classification?','<p>The organism should be <strong>unicellular and eukaryotic</strong>. Locomotion and autotrophic nutrition alone are not sufficient because different organisms can show these features. The chapter uses unicellularity and the presence of a true nucleus as important criteria for Protista.</p>','3',None),
    nq(14,'A researcher identified a unicellular eukaryotic organism as fungi. What identification key would you suggest according to the five kingdom classification to keep a unicellular organism in the Kingdom Fungi?','<p>Look for fungal characteristics such as a <strong>chitin cell wall</strong> and heterotrophic nutrition by absorption. The chapter uses yeast as an example of a unicellular organism placed in Fungi because it has fungal characteristics.</p>','3',None),
    nq(15,'<p>During a long-term ecological study, students examined organisms collected from three different environments\u2009—\u2009a freshwater pond, damp soil near decaying logs and the digestive tract of animals. Instead of naming organisms directly, scientists recorded only structural, cellular and nutritional features as given in the table below.</p><table class="sj-table"><thead><tr><th>Organisms</th><th>Key Observations</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P</td><td>Microscopic; no true nucleus; rigid cell covering; survives high salinity and temperature</td></tr><tr><td>Q</td><td>Multicellular; filamentous body; cell wall present; no chlorophyll; grows on dead organic matter</td></tr><tr><td>R</td><td>Unicellular; true nucleus; contractile vacuole present; moves using flagella; shows photosynthesis in light but heterotrophic in the absence of light</td></tr><tr><td>S</td><td>Multicellular; well-differentiated tissues; backbone present; aquatic respiration during early life stage</td></tr><tr><td>T</td><td>Acellular; contains genetic material; remains inactive outside a host cell</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The students realised that some organisms fit neatly into Whittaker’s five kingdom classification, while others challenged the very basis of this classification.</p><p><strong>Based on the case study, answer the following questions\u2009—</strong></p><ol type="i"><li>Identify one organism that clearly belongs to the Kingdom Fungi. State one observation that supports your answer.</li><li>Which organism would be placed in the Kingdom Monera? Mention one characteristic that justifies this placement.</li><li>Organisms R and Q are both eukaryotic, yet they are placed in different kingdoms. Analyse the criteria that separate them.</li><li>Explain why organism S cannot be classified using the mode of nutrition alone.</li><li>Organism T does not fit into any of the five kingdoms. Which fundamental characteristic used in classification does it lack and what does this reveal about the limitations of classification systems?</li></ol>','<p><strong>(i)</strong> Q — Fungi. It is multicellular, filamentous, has a cell wall, lacks chlorophyll and grows on dead organic matter.</p><p><strong>(ii)</strong> P — Monera. It has no true nucleus, so it is prokaryotic.</p><p><strong>(iii)</strong> R is unicellular and eukaryotic with mixed nutrition and locomotion, fitting Protista. Q is multicellular, filamentous and heterotrophic by absorption, fitting Fungi.</p><p><strong>(iv)</strong> Mode of nutrition alone cannot classify S because many organisms are heterotrophic. Its multicellularity, differentiated tissues and backbone provide important structural criteria.</p><p><strong>(v)</strong> T lacks cellular organisation because it is acellular. This shows that a classification system based on cellular organisation has limits when organisms fall outside those criteria.</p>','5'),
]


